#Objective
Configure a protection system against :
•	brute-force attacks,
•	repeated login attempts,
•	malicious SSH access.
Using : Fail2ban.


#Context
In professional environments :
•	SSH servers are constantly scanned,
•	brute-force attacks are frequent,
•	malicious authentication attempts must be automatically blocked.
Fail2ban monitors :
•	system logs,
•	detects authentication failures,
•	automatically bans malicious IPs.


#Protection Architecture
| Step | Action               |
| ---- | -------------------- |
| 1    | Analyze logs         |
| 2    | Detect SSH fails     |
| 3    | IP ban               |
| 4    | Temporary blockage   |


#Security Policy
| Parameter         | Value      |
| ----------------- | ---------- |
| Service Protected | SSH        |
| Max Retries       | 3          |
| Ban Time          | 10 minutes |
| Find Time         | 10 minutes |


#Verify Installaiton
sudo systemctl status fail2ban --no-pager
sudo apt install fail2ban -y
fail2ban-client --version


#Backup Configuration
sudo cp /etc/fail2ban/jail.conf /etc/fail2ban/jail.conf.bak


#Create jail.local
sudo nano /etc/fail2ban/jail.local
[DEFAULT]
bantime = 10m
findtime = 10m
maxretry = 3
backend = systemd
[sshd]
enabled = true
port = ssh
logpath = %(sshd_log)s


#Explanations
| Directive | Purpose                    |
| --------- | -------------------------- |
| bantime   | Ban duration               |
| findtime  | Analysis window            |
| maxretry  | Maximum number of failures |
| enabled   | Activate SSH protection    |


#Restart Fail2ban
sudo fail2ban-client -t
sudo systemctl restart fail2ban
sudo systemctl status fail2ban --no-pager


#Verify SSH Jail
sudo fail2ban-client status
sudo fail2ban-client status sshd

